  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>State Space and Trajectories</H2>
    <Body>
**State space** is the universe of all possible states your system can be in. For a single neuron model with just voltage, state space is the real line: every point corresponds to a possible voltage. For a more realistic model tracking voltage $V$ and some gating variable $n$, state space is the 2D plane where each point is a $(V, n)$ pair.

A **trajectory** is the path your system traces through state space as time flows. Start at initial condition $x_0$, follow the differential equation, and you get a curve $x(t)$ that shows where you are at every moment.

Think of it like this: if you're hiking in mountains (state space), your current position is the state. The slope of the terrain tells you which way to walk (the vector field $f(x, t)$). Your trajectory is the actual path you take. Where you end up depends on where you started and what the terrain looks like.

For neurons, different initial voltages produce different trajectories, but — here's the magic — many trajectories converge to the same final destination. That's because of **attractors**.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-state-space">
    <Front>What is state space in a dynamical system?</Front>
    <Back>The universe of all possible states the system can occupy. Each point represents a complete specification of the system's configuration at an instant.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <FlashCard id="fc-trajectory">
    <Front>What is a trajectory in a dynamical system?</Front>
    <Back>The path a system traces through state space over time as it evolves according to its differential equation $dx/dt = f(x, t)$.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Mermaid>
graph LR
    A[Initial State x0] -->|Evolve via dx/dt = f| B[State x1 at t1]
    B --> C[State x2 at t2]
    C --> D[...]
    D --> E[Final State / Attractor]
    style A fill:#e1f5ff
    style E fill:#ffe1e1
</Mermaid>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Deterministic vs. Stochastic Dynamics</H2>
    <Body>
Real neurons are noisy. Ion channels open and close randomly. Synaptic inputs arrive unpredictably. Should we throw determinism out the window?

Not yet. We distinguish two regimes:

**Deterministic systems**: The equation $dx/dt = f(x, t)$ completely determines the future. Same initial condition always produces the same trajectory. This works when noise is negligible compared to the driving forces.

**Stochastic systems**: Add a random term:

$$dx = f(x, t) \, dt + \sigma \, dW(t)$$

Now $dW(t)$ is a Wiener process (Brownian motion), and $\sigma$ controls noise intensity. Trajectories become *probabilistic* — you get a different path every time, even from the same starting point.

When does noise matter? Compare the noise strength to the distance between attractors. If you're studying how a neuron settles to rest ($\sim$10 mV from threshold), and your noise is 0.1 mV, treat it as deterministic. If noise is 5 mV and threshold is only 15 mV away, you need stochastic analysis.

For this lesson, we focus on deterministic foundations. Once you understand attractors without noise, adding stochasticity (Module 3) becomes a controlled extension.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-deterministic">
    <Front>What defines a deterministic dynamical system?</Front>
    <Back>A system where the governing equation $dx/dt = f(x, t)$ completely determines the future trajectory from any initial condition, with no random components.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Attractors: Where Trajectories End Up</H2>
    <Body>
Here's the most important concept in this lesson: most trajectories don't wander forever. They settle down to predictable long-term behaviors called **attractors**.

An **attractor** is a set of states toward which nearby trajectories converge. Once you reach an attractor, you stay there (or move within it if it's more complex than a single point).

Three types you'll see in neuroscience:

1. **Fixed point attractor**: A single state. All nearby trajectories spiral in and stop. Example: resting potential $V_{rest}$. No matter where you start (within a range), voltage decays back to $V_{rest}$.

2. **Limit cycle**: A closed loop in state space. Trajectories spiral onto the loop and keep circulating. Example: rhythmic spiking neurons. The voltage-gating variable trajectory traces an oval over and over.

3. **Strange attractor**: A fractal structure with chaotic dynamics. Rare in single neurons, but shows up in some network models.

For most single neuron models, fixed points dominate. Understanding them unlocks stability analysis, bifurcations, and why neurons have distinct "modes" (quiescent, tonic spiking, bursting).
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-attractor">
    <Front>What is an attractor in a dynamical system?</Front>
    <Back>A set of states toward which trajectories converge and remain once reached. Fixed points, limit cycles, and strange attractors are the three main types.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

  <Mermaid>
graph TD
    subgraph "Fixed Point Attractor"
    A1[Initial state] -->|Trajectory| A2[Approaching fixed point]
    A2 --> A3[Settles at equilibrium]
    end
    subgraph "Limit Cycle Attractor"
    B1[Initial state] -->|Trajectory spirals| B2[Onto periodic orbit]
    B2 -->|Repeats| B2
    end
    subgraph "Strange Attractor"
    C1[Initial state] -->|Chaotic trajectory| C2[Bounded unpredictable motion]
    C2 --> C2
    end
</Mermaid>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Fixed Points and Attractive Basins</H2>
    <Body>
A **fixed point** (or equilibrium) is a state $x^*$ where $f(x^*, t) = 0$. The derivative vanishes, so if you're exactly at $x^*$, you stay there forever.

But not all fixed points are attracting. Stability matters:

- **Stable (attracting) fixed point**: Nearby trajectories converge toward it. Mathematically, the eigenvalues of the Jacobian $\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}$ evaluated at $x^*$ have negative real parts.

- **Unstable fixed point**: Nearby trajectories flee away. Positive eigenvalues mean exponential divergence.

The **attractive basin** (or basin of attraction) is the set of all initial conditions that eventually reach a particular attractor. Picture a landscape: attractors are valleys, basins are the drainage areas feeding into them. Initial conditions on a ridge might roll into different valleys depending on tiny perturbations.

For the leaky integrator neuron model:

$$\frac{dV}{dt} = -\frac{V - V_{rest}}{\tau}$$

The fixed point is at $V^* = V_{rest}$ (since $dV/dt = 0$ there). Is it stable? The derivative $\frac{df}{dV} = -1/\tau &lt; 0$, so yes — exponential decay toward rest. The basin is the entire real line: every initial voltage eventually returns to $V_{rest}$.

This is why your resting potential is robust. You can inject current, push voltage up or down, but once the stimulus ends, the system "remembers" where it belongs and goes back.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <Section type="example">
    <H3>Worked Example: Finding the Fixed Point</H3>
    <Body>
Let's analyze the simplest neuronal model:

$$\frac{dV}{dt} = -\frac{V - V_{rest}}{\tau}$$

Assume $V_{rest} = -70$ mV and $\tau = 10$ ms.

**Step 1**: Find the fixed point by setting $dV/dt = 0$:

$$0 = -\frac{V^* - (-70)}{10}$$

This gives $V^* = -70$ mV. The resting potential is the fixed point.

**Step 2**: Check stability. Compute the derivative of the right-hand side with respect to $V$:

$$\frac{\partial}{\partial V}\left(-\frac{V - V_{rest}}{\tau}\right) = -\frac{1}{\tau} = -\frac{1}{10} = -0.1$$

Since this is negative, the fixed point is **stable**.

**Step 3**: Solve the ODE analytically. Starting from $V(0) = V_0$:

$$V(t) = V_{rest} + (V_0 - V_{rest}) e^{-t/\tau}$$

If $V_0 = -50$ mV (depolarized start):

$$V(t) = -70 + (-50 + 70) e^{-t/10} = -70 + 20 e^{-t/10}$$

At $t = 0$: $V = -50$ mV. At $t = 10$ ms: $V \approx -70 + 20e^{-1} \approx -62.6$ mV. As $t \to \infty$, $V \to -70$ mV.

Every trajectory decays exponentially toward the fixed point, proving it's a global attractor.
    </Body>
    <Code lang="python">
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# Parameters
V_rest = -70  # mV
tau = 10      # ms
V0 = -50      # initial voltage

# Time vector
t = np.linspace(0, 50, 500)

# Analytical solution
V = V_rest + (V0 - V_rest) * np.exp(-t / tau)

# Plot trajectory
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.plot(t, V, label=f'V(t) from V0={V0} mV', linewidth=2)
plt.axhline(V_rest, color='r', linestyle='--', label='Fixed point (V_rest)')
plt.xlabel('Time (ms)')
plt.ylabel('Voltage (mV)')
plt.title('Trajectory Converging to Fixed Point Attractor')
plt.legend()
plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
plt.show()
</Code>
  </Section>

  <Section type="concept">
    <H2>Biological Interpretation: Resting Potential as an Attractor</H2>
    <Body>
Why do neurons have a stable resting potential? It's not magic — it's dynamical systems.

At rest, the membrane has leak channels (mostly potassium) that create a conductance $g_L$. The driving force $(V - V_{rest})$ pushes voltage toward $V_{rest}$, which is the reversal potential weighted by channel permeabilities (Nernst-Goldman equation).

The **resting state is a fixed point attractor**. If you depolarize the neuron (push $V$ up), leak channels pull it back down. If you hyperpolarize it (push $V$ down), the driving force flips and pulls it back up. The membrane time constant $\tau = C / g_L$ sets how fast this happens.

This explains several phenomena:

- **Robustness**: Small perturbations (synaptic noise, measurement artifacts) don't permanently change the resting voltage. The attractor pulls the system back.

- **Threshold dynamics**: If you push $V$ far enough (above threshold), you might escape the basin of the resting attractor and enter a different dynamical regime (spiking). More on this in Lesson 1.2.

- **Pathology**: If ion channel expression changes (disease, drugs), $V_{rest}$ shifts. The attractor moves, and the neuron's baseline behavior changes permanently until channels are restored.

The geometric view — neuron as point in state space, rest as attractor — is not metaphor. It's the actual mathematical structure governing the biophysics.
    </Body>
  </Section>

  <FlashCard id="fc-resting-potential">
    <Front>Why is the neuronal resting potential stable?</Front>
    <Back>It's a stable fixed point attractor. Leak currents create a restoring force proportional to deviation from rest, pulling voltage back exponentially with time constant $\tau = C/g_L$.</Back>
  </FlashCard>

